# November 21, 2026: central Sullivan County, New York (region cat, drawn at random by tools/choose.py --claim --state ny,
# which offered Onteora Park, Monticello and New Kingston; the Atlas had nothing in Monticello, Liberty, Woodbourne,
# Hurleyville, Glen Wild, Mountain Dale, Bethel or Grahamsville). The county seat and Liberty's downtown, the Woodstock
# festival field at Bethel, the Fallsburg tunnel of the Ontario & Western, an 1815 stone house where a murder ended a
# land feud, the D&H Canal's Silver Lake feeder dam, the Borscht Belt synagogues, theatres and resort colony, and the
# Greek Revival inns and churches of Woodbourne and Grahamsville.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py, including the Sullivan County Register
# list); the Hasbrouck Stone House Register form (2019) through NPGallery; and through tools/page.py the Sullivan O&W Rail
# Trail pages, Sullivan County's page for the museum in Hurleyville, the Sullivan County Historical Society, Bethel
# Woods' visit page, the National Park Service's page for the Woodstock site, HMdb, courthouses.co, WAMC, the River
# Reporter, Radio Catskill and cinematreasures.org. Most Register forms for this county are NPGallery placeholders.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HM(m,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+m]
OW=L('Sullivan O&W Rail Trail: history','https://sullivanoandw.com/history/')

BATCH={'key':'cat_ycxn','label':'Central Sullivan County, NY: Monticello, Liberty, Bethel, Woodbourne and the Borscht Belt hamlets','added':'2026-11-21 09:43'}

PLACES=[
N('hasbrouck-stone-house-fallsburg','Hasbrouck Stone House ("the Old Stone House"), Hasbrouck','Hasbrouck, Town of Fallsburg (Woodbourne post office)','cat','landmark','standing',41.79215,-74.61646,'site','view','drive','','c. 1815',
  'A large rubble-stone house in the Federal style, built about 1815 for Anthony Hasbrouck (1788-1840), one of the richest men in the county at a time when about a quarter of the town still lived in log houses. In 1840 Hasbrouck was murdered in this house by Cornelius Hardenbergh, the end of a feud over land and inheritance (Hardenbergh\'s own grandfather had been murdered in 1808 over disputed titles); Hardenbergh was the first person in Sullivan County sentenced to hang. Later the house took summer boarders, into the 1970s, and its frame additions were then taken off. The Concerned Citizens of Hasbrouck bought it after 1976 and it was listed on the National Register in 2019.',
  'A two-part stone house: a gable-roofed main block with a centre hall, and a story-and-a-half wing beside it with its ridge parallel. The Register form says much of the c. 1815 trim survives inside, with Federal moulded door and window casings and the original open-string staircase. The form lists its present use as recreation and culture (a community building); interior not checked and no opening times found.',
  'The house where Sullivan County\'s first death sentence began, with a feud over land titles.',
  'Owned by the Concerned Citizens of Hasbrouck (Register form). At 282 Hasbrouck Road; no public opening hours were found, so look from the road. Not checked on the ground.',
  [L('National Register form: Hasbrouck Stone House (NPGallery PDF)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/8adc2626-042b-433d-b4ea-e180ffc1fabd'),L('NPGallery record 100004419','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/100004419')],
  brick='?'),
N('woodstock-festival-site-bethel','Woodstock Music Festival site and the 1984 monument, Bethel','Bethel','cat','landmark','marker',41.70245,-74.88166,'exact','public','drive','','1969',
  'On August 15 to 18, 1969, the Woodstock Music and Art Fair drew an estimated 450,000 people to Max Yasgur\'s dairy farm here, on nearly 300 acres of farmland, with 32 acts from Richie Havens to Jimi Hendrix. A monument went up at the edge of the field in 1984. In 1996 the Gerry Foundation bought the 37-acre festival field and land round it, and the Bethel Woods Center for the Arts opened beside it in 2006, with its museum of the festival and the 1960s (Wikipedia gives the museum\'s opening as both 2006 and June 2008). The site went on the National Register in 2017.',
  'The bowl-shaped festival hill itself, kept as a field, and the 1984 monument with its plaques at the corner of West Shore Road and Hurd Road. The Bethel Woods marker there says the stage stood to the left as you face the hill. The museum is up Hurd Road on the Bethel Woods campus.',
  'Stand on the hill where half a million people came in 1969.',
  'The field and the arts centre are owned and run by Bethel Woods Center for the Arts (NPS page: managed by the Museum at Bethel Woods). A small pull-off lot by the monument on West Shore Road; the museum has its own lots on Hurd Road. Museum hours from bethelwoodscenter.org/visit; tickets for the museum; the field may be closed on concert and event days (not checked).',
  [W('Bethel Woods Center for the Arts'),L('NPS: Woodstock Music Festival Site','https://www.nps.gov/places/woodstock-music-festival-site.htm'),HM('310116','Woodstock Festival Monument'),L('Bethel Woods: visit','https://www.bethelwoodscenter.org/visit')],
  th=[41.70272,-74.88174],thn='Pull-off by the monument, West Shore Road',brick='no',
  hours='Museum at Bethel Woods: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (the field and monument are outdoors)'),
N('silver-lake-dam-woodridge','Silver Lake Dam (D&H Canal feeder), Woodridge','Village of Woodridge, Town of Fallsburg','cat','canal','standing',41.701667,-74.550833,'site','view','drive','canal','1840s',
  'Built in the 1840s to hold back Sandburg Creek and send a steady supply of water to the summit level of the Delaware and Hudson Canal about 10 miles to the southeast, when the canal was enlarged. It was rebuilt about 1895, as the canal was winding down. The 85-acre pond it made, later Silver Lake, became an attraction for the Jewish summer resorts round Woodridge. The village owns the dam and has worked in this century to repair a leak, with the state holding up the work while it weighed the dam\'s hazard rating.',
  'A stone dam 176 feet long and 13 feet high on the downstream side, of large mortared slabs round a dry rubble core, with a central spillway and two cast-iron waste pipes worked by gate valves in its top (Wikipedia, from the Register listing). Whether it can be seen from Silver Lake Road was not checked.',
  'A canal company\'s dam that turned into a resort lake.',
  'Owned by the Village of Woodridge (Wikipedia). Off Silver Lake Road outside the village; no public access path found, so look from the road. The Mountaindale O&W Rail Trail lot is about 850 m southeast. Not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Silver Lake Dam'),W('Silver Lake (Woodridge, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('sullivan-county-museum-hurleyville','Sullivan County Museum (Hurleyville school of 1912), Hurleyville','Hurleyville, Town of Fallsburg','cat','civic','standing',41.73932,-74.67305,'exact','open','drive','','1912',
  'Built in 1912 as the Hurleyville elementary school, later its high school; the last class graduated in 1945, when the county\'s small schools were centralised. After several owners the county bought it, and the Sullivan County Historical Society (founded 1886) moved in in November 1972. It also holds the Frederick A. Cook Society\'s collection on the polar explorer.',
  'A three-story former school; the society says that apart from an elevator and modest changes inside it keeps its old character. Exhibits on county history and an archive for research. The Hurleyville section of the O&W rail trail (the "Milk Train Trail") passes the hamlet.',
  'A 1912 schoolhouse that now keeps the county\'s past.',
  'Owned and kept by Sullivan County (Department of Parks, Recreation and Beautification), at 265 Main Street; hours from the county\'s page. Parking on Main Street and at the rail-trail lots south of the building.',
  [L('Sullivan County: Cultural Center (Museum)','https://sullivanny.gov/node/733'),L('Sullivan County Historical Society: about','https://www.scnyhistory.org/about/'),L('Sullivan O&W Rail Trail: Hurleyville','https://sullivanoandw.com/hurleyville/')],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; closed Sunday and Monday'),
N('fallsburg-tunnel-ow','Fallsburg Tunnel (Ontario & Western Railway), South Fallsburg','South Fallsburg, Town of Fallsburg','cat','rail','ruins',41.70181,-74.62131,'exact','public','walk','','1886–1957',
  'A brick railroad tunnel 1,123 feet long, built in 1886 on the main line of the New York, Ontario & Western Railway, which carried summer crowds from New York City to the Sullivan County resorts and milk and coal the other way. The O&W went bankrupt and was sold off in 1957, the first Class I railroad in the country to be abandoned whole. The tunnel is now partly collapsed.',
  'The northwest portal, seen from the end of the Tunnel Bypass Trail, which climbs over Tunnel Hill instead of using the bore. The trail operator says some of the steel plates holding up the tunnel have partly collapsed: going inside is prohibited and dangerous. View the portal from the trail.',
  'An 1886 brick tunnel on the first big railroad in America to be abandoned entirely.',
  'The 1.1-mile Tunnel Bypass Trail is a public section of the Sullivan O&W Rail Trail (the rail-trail alliance\'s page; owner of the ground not checked). Start at the lot near Old Country Road and Dogpatch Road (Dogpatch Town Park, off Lake Street; the trail page also gives 29 Skunk Hollow Road). Hilly, unpaved; open daylight hours only, no motor vehicles or hunting, pets leashed (trail page). Do not enter the tunnel.',
  [L('Sullivan O&W Rail Trail: South Fallsburg (Tunnel Bypass Trail)','https://sullivanoandw.com/south-fallsburg/'),OW],
  th=[41.70342,-74.62197],thn='Lot off Old Country Road (Dogpatch Town Park)',brick='?',hours='Trail open daylight hours only'),
N('munson-diner-liberty','Munson Diner (New Munson Diner), Liberty','Village of Liberty','cat','civic','standing',41.79714,-74.74458,'exact','view','drive','','1945',
  'A stainless-steel diner made in 1945 by the Kullman Dining Car Company of Lebanon, New Jersey, that stood at 11th Avenue and 49th Street in Manhattan, where it was a film location (Kojak, and "Reggie\'s" in Seinfeld). Facing demolition, it was trucked 90 miles to Liberty in 2005 with the help of Allan Berube, and listed on the National Register in 2006. It stood empty for two years before a family from the Bronx reopened it, about 2017, as the New Munson Diner (River Reporter, 2019).',
  'A 16-by-50-foot diner on a riveted steel frame, sided in stainless steel and porcelain enamel, with a 1940s counter-and-booth plan inside. Whether it is serving now was not checked.',
  'A Manhattan diner from Seinfeld, now on a hill in the Catskills.',
  'Private business, overlooking Main Street in Liberty. Opening hours not checked; look from the street if closed.',
  [W('Munson Diner'),L('River Reporter: The New Munson Diner (2019)','https://riverreporter.com/stories/the-new-munson-diner-its-all-in-the-family,16440')],
  brick='no'),
N('liberty-downtown-historic-district','Liberty Downtown Historic District','Village of Liberty','cat','town','standing',41.80083,-74.74667,'area','public','walk','','',
  'The commercial core of Liberty, listed on the National Register in 1978 (12 buildings) and enlarged in 2006 to 112 contributing buildings; the historian Allan Berube helped bring about the larger district. Two of its buildings have their own listings: the Town and Country Building, a meeting hall and store of about 1890 that was the Lyric Theatre before 1913, given a "googie" front about 1950 and home for 50 years to a men\'s clothing store, and the Munson Diner on the hill above Main Street.',
  'Main Street\'s storefront blocks; the Town and Country Building with its 1950s front; the Methodist church pictured in the Wikipedia article; St. Paul\'s Lutheran Church round the corner. Pin is the Register point for the district.',
  'A Catskill resort town\'s Main Street, saved block by block.',
  'Public streets in the Village of Liberty; street parking.',
  [W('Liberty Downtown Historic District (Liberty, New York)'),W('Town and Country Building')],
  brick='?'),
N('st-pauls-lutheran-church-liberty','St. Paul\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church, Liberty','Village of Liberty','cat','worship','standing',41.79917,-74.74701,'exact','view','drive','','1908',
  'A modest Late Gothic Revival church built in 1908, listed on the National Register in 1997.',
  'Iron-spot brick with limestone and terra-cotta trim, a gabled nave and an engaged tower at the side holding the entrance. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church on Chestnut Street; no service times found on a page of its own, so look from the street.',
  [W('St. Paul\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church (Liberty, New York)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('sullivan-county-courthouse-monticello','Sullivan County Courthouse, Monticello','Village of Monticello','cat','civic','standing',41.65556,-74.68472,'exact','view','drive','','1909–1910',
  'Monticello has been the county seat since Sullivan County was made in 1809. The present courthouse was built in 1909-1910 for $142,800 to designs by William J. Beardsley of Poughkeepsie, replacing one of 1845; it was renovated in 1979 and is named the Lawrence H. Cooke Sullivan County Courthouse. The village\'s Broadway was nominated as a Register historic district in 2019 for its early 20th-century buildings (WAMC).',
  'A two-story building of gray Ohio limestone facing south, with a portico of four tall columns under a pediment and a green dome with a large clock on the roof. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Working county courthouse at Broadway and Bank Street; look from the street. Street parking on Broadway.',
  [L('courthouses.co: Sullivan County','https://courthouses.co/?p=44541'),L('WAMC: part of Monticello could become a historic district (2019)','https://www.wamc.org/post/part-sullivan-county-village-could-become-historic-district')],
  brick='no'),
N('rialto-theatre-monticello','Rialto Theatre block (Miller and Washington Block), Monticello','Village of Monticello','cat','civic','standing',41.65528,-74.68722,'exact','view','drive','','1921',
  'A commercial block of 1921 on Broadway with three storefronts, a restaurant filling the second floor, and the Rialto movie theatre behind. The theatre auditorium, 136 feet deep and of parged concrete, was demolished in 2003, and the marquee was pulled down and junked in 2012. Listed on the National Register in 2001.',
  'The two-story, six-bay brick front block on Broadway still stands; the old lobby is a storefront. Nothing of the auditorium is left. Interior not checked.',
  'A movie palace\'s front, with the theatre gone from behind it.',
  'Private storefronts on Broadway; look from the street.',
  [W('Rialto Theater (Monticello, New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('st-johns-episcopal-church-monticello','St. John\'s Episcopal Church and Rectory, Monticello','Village of Monticello','cat','worship','standing',41.65346,-74.68511,'exact','view','drive','','1879–1881',
  'An Episcopal church and rectory at 15 St. John\'s Street, built 1879-1881 in the Gothic Revival style in an L of church and attached chapel. Listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'Quarry-faced, randomly laid coursed stone, with a tall three-stage entrance tower at the corner topped by battlements. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active parish; no service times found on a page of its own, so look from the street.',
  [W('St. John\'s Episcopal Church and Rectory (Monticello, New York)')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('center-theatre-woodbourne','Center Theatre (Woodbourne Theater), Woodbourne','Woodbourne, Town of Fallsburg','cat','civic','ruins',41.75833,-74.60222,'exact','view','drive','','1938',
  'An Art Deco movie theatre of 1938, built for the summer crowds of the Borscht Belt and listed on the National Register in 2001. It is closed: a visitor writing on Cinema Treasures saw the front boarded with plywood, an old demolition permit posted at the rear, and the auditorium collapsed or partly demolished behind it.',
  'A three-bay, two-story entrance pavilion (facade, foyer and lobby) with a long auditorium behind, three times as long as it is wide (Wikipedia). Condition from one visitor\'s comment, not checked on the ground.',
  'An Art Deco Borscht Belt movie house left to fall in.',
  'Private and closed; look from State Route 52 only. Do not go in: the visitor reported the auditorium had collapsed.',
  [W('Center Theatre (Woodbourne, New York)'),L('Cinema Treasures: Center Theatre','https://cinematreasures.org/theaters/18721')],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('woodbourne-shul','B\'nai Israel Synagogue (the Woodbourne Shul), Woodbourne','Woodbourne, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.75819,-74.60209,'site','view','drive','','1920',
  'A wood-frame Orthodox synagogue of 1920 at 457 State Route 52, listed on the National Register in 1999. In 2010 Rabbi Mordechai Jungreis, Rebbe of the Nikolsburg-Woodbourne Hasidic dynasty, bought the declining building, and it became one of the busiest synagogues in the Catskills in summer, with prayer groups starting every ten minutes and about 120,000 visits in the summer of 2015 (Wikipedia).',
  'Two stories over a shallow concrete basement, three bays wide and four deep, under a steep gable roof with a deep wooden cornice. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active synagogue, busiest from Memorial Day to Labor Day; look from Route 52 and respect worship.',
  [W('B\'nai Israel Synagogue (Woodbourne, New York)')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('woodbourne-reformed-church','Woodbourne Reformed Church, chapel and cemetery','Woodbourne, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.76191,-74.59944,'exact','view','drive','','1837',
  'A Dutch Reformed church of 1837, enlarged in 1848, when an 18-foot addition gave it a Greek Revival temple front. The present bell tower dates from 1893. The chapel beside it was built in 1849 as a public school and bought by the church in 1891. Listed on the National Register with its cemetery in 2003.',
  'A heavy timber-framed church in clapboard with Doric columns across the front and a two-stage tower with a tall spire; the old schoolhouse chapel and the burying ground. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church on State Route 42; no service times found on a page of its own. The cemetery can be seen from the road.',
  [W('Woodbourne Reformed Church Complex')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('mountaindale-synagogue','Hebrew Congregation of Mountaindale Synagogue and mikvah, Mountain Dale','Mountain Dale, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.68856,-74.53025,'exact','view','drive','','1917',
  'A small Orthodox synagogue at 6 Spring Glen Road, built in 1917 for $5,000 and slightly enlarged in the 1930s; a ritual bath (mikvah) was built beside it as a community project about ten years later, the only synagogue mikvah in the county. Some of the original interior paintings were painted over then. A truck accident in 2009 brought down wires that set the roof alight, doing slight damage. Listed on the National Register, with the mikvah, in 2001.',
  'A three-bay, two-story stucco-faced frame building on a fieldstone base, with a round-arched window bearing a Star of David in the front gable and a later one-story vestibule. Wikipedia describes a heavily marbleised sanctuary with a central bimah and a women\'s gallery. The mikvah stands east of the building. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Congregation\'s property; look from Spring Glen Road. The Mountain Dale O&W Rail Trail trailhead (replica depot, parking) is at 11 Railroad Avenue in the hamlet.',
  [W('Hebrew Congregation of Mountaindale Synagogue'),L('Sullivan O&W Rail Trail: Mountain Dale','https://sullivanoandw.com/mountain-dale/')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('anshei-glen-wild-synagogue','Anshei Glen Wild Synagogue (now an art space), Glen Wild','Glen Wild, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.65,-74.588889,'site','view','drive','','1923',
  'A small Orthodox synagogue built in 1923 by a congregation founded in 1913 by a local family; it never had a rabbi of its own. It was kept almost as built and listed on the National Register in 1999. About 2013 the artist Mike Osterhout bought it and made it an art gallery and performance space; he also owns the 1867 Glen Wild Methodist Church nearby.',
  'One story, stucco-sided, with a porch under a bell-shaped roof carrying the synagogue\'s name and a Star of David, round-arched windows of coloured and opaque glass, and panelled doors under a coloured fanlight. Wikipedia describes a barrel-vaulted sanctuary with the central bimah and curved-end pews. No opening times found; interior not checked.',
  'A family synagogue of the Borscht Belt turned art gallery.',
  'Private, on Glen Wild Road (County Route 58). No public hours found; look from the road. Pin is the Register point, not checked against the building.',
  [W('Anshei Glen Wild Synagogue')],
  brick='no'),
N('glen-wild-methodist-church','Glen Wild Methodist Church ("Church of the Little Green Man"), Glen Wild','Glen Wild, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.666111,-74.588333,'site','view','drive','','1867',
  'A wooden country church built in 1867 in a plain Gothic Revival style, with a church hall of 1944 beside it; listed on the National Register in 1984. It is now owned by the artist Mike Osterhout, who renamed it the Church of the Little Green Man (Wikipedia, in the article on the Glen Wild synagogue).',
  'Board-and-batten siding and a square tower with belfry and steeple at the front; the small L-shaped hall. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private, on Old Glen Wild Road; look from the road.',
  [W('Glen Wild Methodist Church'),W('Anshei Glen Wild Synagogue')],
  brick='no'),
N('masten-quinn-house-wurtsboro','Masten-Quinn House, Wurtsboro','Village of Wurtsboro, Town of Mamakating','cat','landmark','standing',41.576944,-74.481111,'site','view','drive','canal','1820s',
  'A Greek Revival farmhouse built in two phases in the 1820s by Lawrence Masten, whose land the Delaware and Hudson Canal crossed from 1825; the farm was worked until the mid-20th century. In 1995 the land was sold to a developer and Michael Roosa bought the house on its half-acre and saved it from demolition. Listed on the National Register in 2003, the first listing in the village.',
  'A story-and-a-half clapboard house, five bays by two, with a fieldstone and brick chimney and a full-length veranda, by Willsey Brook on First Street; a shagbark hickory to its southeast helped date it. Wikipedia says the dry, overgrown canal bed lies in the woods about 100 m east. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private house on First Street; look from the street.',
  [W('Masten-Quinn House')],
  brick='?'),
N('mamakating-park-historic-district','Mamakating Park, a Victorian summer colony on the ridge','Town of Mamakating (near Wurtsboro)','cat','town','standing',41.6125,-74.536667,'area','view','drive','hotels','1893',
  'In 1893 the Sullivan County Club bought 4,600 acres including Masten Lake, once a D&H Canal feeder reservoir, to build a cottage colony round a big hotel on the highest ground in Mamakating, reached from the Ontario & Western. The Panic of 1893 stopped it after the hotel and about 20 lots were built. A second company ran it from 1902; later the Mamakating Park Inn was a Borscht Belt hotel. The hotel closed in the 1950s, Camp Lakota used the buildings, and the hotel burned in the 1970s. Listed on the National Register in 1998.',
  'About 48 buildings, mostly late Victorian and Shingle-style cottages as built, round the horseshoe of Columbian Road, Mamakating Avenue and Park Road, with the open hotel site and its tennis courts in the middle (Wikipedia). Pin is the Register point for the district.',
  'A grand resort plan that stopped after twenty cottages.',
  'Private homes on town roads; look from the road only, and do not stop on lawns or drives.',
  [W('Mamakating Park Historic District')],
  brick='no'),
N('c-burton-hotel-grahamsville','C. Burton Hotel (Sycamore House), near Grahamsville','Town of Neversink','cat','hotel','standing',41.85889,-74.57694,'area','view','drive','hotels','1851',
  'C. Burton built a small roadside tavern here in 1851 and tripled it two years later; the hotel took travellers and then summer guests who came by stage from the Ontario & Western at Fallsburg, and from 1898 the Grahamsville Fair was held behind it. Later a house and a medical office, it was unoccupied when listed on the National Register in 2005; the owners since 1994 had been restoring it.',
  'A two-story clapboard Greek Revival building, six bays by two, with a recessed front porch of big fluted Doric columns, a ballroom upstairs and marble mantels. A former owner floored the porch with marble from tombstones left behind when the Rondout Reservoir was flooded (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'A Greek Revival inn with a porch paved in tombstones from a drowned valley.',
  'Private, on State Route 55 about a mile west of Grahamsville; look from the road. Wikipedia\'s coordinate and the Register list\'s disagree by about 2 km: see the pin note.',
  [W('C. Burton Hotel')],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('grahamsville-historic-district','Grahamsville Historic District and Reformed Church','Grahamsville, Town of Neversink','cat','town','standing',41.84720,-74.53804,'site','view','drive','','1840s',
  'Five buildings on 20 acres along Route 55 east of the present hamlet. The Grahamsville Reformed Church dates from the 1840s and was enlarged in 1874, when a cemetery was added, after the Reynolds tannery kept the place going through the Panic of 1873. The Marenius Dayton House, a Greek Revival inn, was a stagecoach stop on the road the town built in 1813 from the Neversink to Wawarsing; an 1847 boarding house is an artist\'s gallery. Listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'The Reformed Church across Route 55 from Tri-Valley Central School, its cemetery, and the Greek Revival houses near it. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Church and private houses along State Route 55; look from the road. Time and the Valleys Museum, on the drowned reservoir villages, is at 332 Main Street in the hamlet (season from Memorial Day weekend).',
  [W('Grahamsville Historic District'),L('Time and the Valleys Museum: about','https://www.timeandthevalleysmuseum.org/about-us/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('loch-sheldrake-synagogue','Loch Sheldrake Synagogue (Adas Yisroel), Loch Sheldrake','Loch Sheldrake, Town of Fallsburg','cat','worship','standing',41.770833,-74.658056,'site','view','drive','','1922–1930',
  'An Orthodox synagogue built between 1922 and 1930 on State Route 52 north of Loch Sheldrake Road, listed on the National Register in 1997. Wikipedia says it holds daily services in the summer.',
  'Buff brick on a concrete base, three bays by five, under a steep gable, with a stepped-gable entrance pavilion and a limestone parapet. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active summer congregation; look from Route 52.',
  [W('Loch Sheldrake Synagogue')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
]

